const DETECTOR_CONVENTIONS = `DETECTOR CONVENTIONS (read backend/app/validation/contracts/*.py, backend/app/validation/deterministic/_support.py, backend/app/models/issue.py, backend/app/models/dataset.py, backend/app/models/profile.py, backend/app/profiles/boq.yaml, backend/tests/support/builders.py and backend/tests/unit/validation/test_engine.py first):
- One module per detector: backend/app/validation/deterministic/<name>.py with one class <Name>Detector(Detector[<Name>Config]) and its config class. Spec: detector_id "deterministic.<name>", version "1.0.0", category DETERMINISTIC, baseline=True, cost "low", an honest description, applicability text and limitations, the evidence_classes and issue_categories it can emit, required_fields for the default config (override required_fields(config) when the config chooses the fields). Config: a DetectorConfig subclass where every parameter has a safe default; field ids validated against FIELD_ID_PATTERN; Decimals accept strings; reuse RoundingSpec / TolerancePolicy from validation/contracts/decimal_math.py and FieldConstraints from models/profile.py rather than inventing parallel types; regex parameters must compile (validate them) and may contain at most the documented number of capture groups. The config MUST accept exactly the keys already present for that detector under detector_defaults in backend/app/profiles/boq.yaml, because DetectorRegistry.resolve_config merges profile defaults with plan parameters.
- The contracts were hardened after a design review; read them as they are now: IssueFactory.create(spec, *, rule_id, category, severity, evidence_class, title, explanation, table, row, cells, inputs, observed_value, expected_value, evidence, suggested_action, financial_impact, anomaly_score, fingerprint_key). rule_id is required (one stable snake_case id per rule your detector checks, e.g. "line_total", "minimum", "unique"); cells are the highlighted anchor cells; inputs are the cells a finding was computed from (factors, summands) and are checked by the evidence policy, which downgrades confirmed findings built on unverified (low-confidence OCR) cells and annotates inferred formatting. The factory rejects evidence classes and categories your spec does not declare, so declare every class and category you emit. Fingerprints are computed from source coordinates and the evidence digest from values: never build or modify ValidationIssue objects yourself (the pipeline fails a detector that returns issues the factory did not create, or modified copies). Use FrozenModel.evolve(...) for validated copies, never model_copy(update=...). EvidenceItem has an optional typed calculation (app.models.issue.Calculation / CalculationOperand) that every recomputation must fill (operation, operands with cells, terms, exact_result, rounding description, rounded_result, reported, difference, allowed_difference) so the UI and exports render calculations uniformly. FinancialImpact has kind ("discrepancy" or "potential_double_count"), reported and recomputed; use app.validation.contracts.issue_factory.money_impact.
- Parameters are profile-controlled unless declared with app.validation.contracts.detector.plannable(...): mark only safe choices plannable (which fields to check, severities, caps); tolerances, rounding, regular expressions and on/off switches stay profile-controlled (plain Field). Verbatim digital cells have extraction_confidence None (not 1.0); never print a confidence for them.
- Read data only through ValidationContext (rows(), cell(), number(), number_value(), text(), cell_ref(), column(), has_fields(), profile_field(), and the column-id variants cell_at(), number_at(), number_value_at(), text_at() for unmapped columns; units; currencies) and create issues only via context.issues.create(self.spec, ...). context.rows(table) yields item rows and skips rows a reviewer marked as not data (Row.excluded; never count them anywhere). Row.counts_toward_totals is False for items listed but not added into totals (optional/alternative/"nur EP" items). Row.section_path / Row.section_row_id / Row.closes_section_id and RowClassification.section_level describe the section hierarchy. Arithmetic helpers raise ArithmeticError rather than lose exactness: catch it per row and count the row as skipped. Abstain instead of guessing: unusable inputs (AMBIGUOUS, FAILED, UNAVAILABLE, empty) never produce a confirmed finding; count them in DetectorResult.coverage (e.g. rows_checked, rows_skipped_missing_inputs) and add a note when a check was partly impossible, so silence is never mistaken for a clean result.
- Every issue has: a specific title (<= 200 chars, no internal ids), an explanation naming the table and source row (_support.row_label) and the profile labels of fields (_support.field_label), observed_value / expected_value as plain strings (_support.fmt for decimals), structured evidence items (CALCULATION for recomputations with the exact arithmetic written out, RULE for the rule or constraint applied, VALUE / COMPARISON / REFERENCE / NOTE as appropriate) with cell refs and decimals as strings in data, a concrete suggested_action, affected cells via _support.refs or context.cell_ref (mapped cells only), and a deterministic fingerprint_key whenever one row can produce several findings of the same category (e.g. the field id or the constraint name).
- Financial impact only when it is a real, calculable money difference: amount = stated - recomputed (positive means the document states more), currency only through _support.explicit_currency (None when unmarked or conflicting; never assume a default currency), basis text describing the calculation.
- Evidence classes: confirmed_rule_violation only when a deterministic rule fails on usable inputs; extraction_uncertainty when the source could not be read or structured reliably; needs_external_context when the document alone cannot settle it; semantic_warning for advisory text/unit rules; suspected_anomaly only where explicitly stated below. Never human_confirmed_error, never a probability, never an invented confidence number.
- Group repetitive findings where stated (one issue per column or value group, capped affected cells, the full count in evidence data and explanation) so one systematic problem does not flood the issue register.
- Tests: one module per detector at backend/tests/unit/validation/deterministic/test_<name>.py using DatasetBuilder / make_context / *_cell helpers from tests/support/builders.py and the boq_profile / generic_profile fixtures from tests/conftest.py. Cover positive cases, clean data with zero issues, abstention, tolerance boundaries (exactly at and just beyond), config validation (unknown key rejected, invalid values rejected, the boq.yaml defaults accepted through DetectorRegistry.resolve_config), applicability (skipped with a reason when fields are unmapped), German and English examples, and fingerprint stability (same input, same fingerprints). Add one test asserting that DetectorRegistry.from_packages(["app.validation.deterministic"]) discovers your detector ids.`

const NORMALIZATION = `${SHARED}

YOUR OWNERSHIP: backend/app/normalization/** (implement build_dataset in builder.py, keeping its public signature and NormalizationOptions; you may add options with defaults; add small modules) and backend/tests/unit/normalization/** (the package exists). Nothing else.

Read: backend/app/ingestion/base.py (RawWorkbook / RawSheet / RawCell / MergedRange), backend/app/models/{dataset,values,source,profile}.py, backend/app/profiles/{units,currency,loader}.py, backend/app/profiles/boq.yaml and generic.yaml, backend/app/core/{ids,clock,errors,config}.py. The ingestion adapters are being written in parallel by another engineer: do NOT import them; build RawWorkbook objects directly in your tests (put helpers in a non-test module such as tests/unit/normalization/raw_builders.py).

TASK: RawWorkbook + DomainProfile -> canonical Dataset. Suggested modules: text.py (cleanup and comparison keys), numbers.py (locale-aware number parsing, pure functions), dates.py, headers.py (header row detection), mapping.py (header -> semantic field), cells.py (RawCell -> Cell), types.py (column type profiles), rows.py (row classification and sections), warnings.py (warning codes and the quality rule), builder.py (orchestration only).

1. Sheets: workbook order. Skip hidden sheets (warning hidden_sheet_skipped) and sheets without non-empty cells (empty_sheet_skipped); both informational. One table per sheet in Phase 1 (state this limitation in the module docstring). Table name = sheet name; Table.source = sheet-level SourceLocation. Pass adapter warnings through into ExtractionReport.warnings (sheet warnings with the new table_id) and copy RawWorkbook.details into ExtractionReport.details; adapter_id/adapter_version from the RawWorkbook. Raise app.core.errors.ExtractionError("No table could be extracted …") when no sheet yields a table.

2. Header detection (headers.py) over the first options.header_scan_rows non-empty rows:
   - PROFILE_SYNONYMS: score each candidate row by the number of DISTINCT profile fields its text cells match (same matcher as mapping below). Highest score >= 2 wins; ties go to the earlier row and record warning header_ambiguous. HeaderDetection.matched_fields lists the field ids. Rows above the header are a title block, not data; say so in HeaderDetection.note ("Rows 1-3 above the header were treated as a title block.").
   - Two-row headers: if the row directly below the chosen header holds only text cells and matching it adds at least one field for a column whose upper header cell is empty or unmapped, combine the two rows (header text "upper lower" where both exist, else whichever exists), note "Two-row header (rows N and N+1)", and data starts below the second row. Horizontally merged upper header cells (RawSheet.merged_ranges) apply their text to every spanned column before combining, e.g. "Preis" over "EP" | "GP" gives "Preis EP" | "Preis GP", which must still map to unit_price / line_total (contains matching).
   - STRUCTURAL: else the first row with >= 2 non-empty cells, all text, followed within 3 rows by a row with a numeric cell, where >= 2 of the text cells sit in columns that hold data below.
   - FIRST_ROW: else the first non-empty row if all its non-empty cells are text.
   - NONE: else; columns are named "Column A", "Column B" … and every row is data.
   STRUCTURAL, FIRST_ROW and NONE add warning header_uncertain naming the method. HeaderDetection.header_row_numbers lists the header rows (two for two-row headers, empty for NONE).

3. Columns: one Column per source column with a header text or at least one non-empty data cell, ordered by source column; position 0-based; source_column_number 1-based; original_header exactly as written (None when absent); normalized_name = lower snake_case of the cleaned header ("Unit price (EUR)" -> "unit_price_eur"), "column_<letter>" when absent, made unique with _2, _3 suffixes; header_currency = the single code CurrencyResolver.find_in_text finds in the header (None for none or several), only for columns mapped to a field with role monetary or unmapped columns whose inferred type is NUMBER (so "CAD drawing ref" in a text header is not a currency).

4. Mapping (mapping.py). Comparison key for headers, synonyms, labels and field ids: NFKC, casefold, every character that is not a letter, digit or % becomes a space, whitespace collapsed ("Pos.-Nr." -> "pos nr", "E.P." -> "e p", "Menge [m³]" -> "menge m3"). Methods by priority: HEADER_EXACT (keys equal; similarity 1.0); HEADER_CONTAINS (the synonym key occurs as a whole-token sequence inside the header key, e.g. "unit price eur" contains "unit price"; a single-token synonym must be >= 2 characters; similarity = len(synonym key) / len(header key)); HEADER_FUZZY (difflib.SequenceMatcher ratio of the full keys >= 0.88; keys shorter than 4 characters never fuzzy-match; similarity = the ratio). Similarity is a string-similarity measure, never presented as a probability. Assignment is one-to-one with a deterministic greedy order: method priority, then similarity, then longer synonym, then leftmost column. A column gets at most one field and a field at most one column. When two columns tie for one field (e.g. "Kurztext" and "Langtext" both -> description) the leftmost wins and warning mapping_conflict (informational) names both headers. matched_synonym records the synonym used.
   After parsing: a column mapped to a NUMBER field whose non-empty data cells are less than 50 % numeric (NUMBER, or AMBIGUOUS numeric text) loses its mapping (warning mapping_rejected_type_mismatch). Required fields (profile.required_fields()) still unmapped in a table: warning required_field_unmapped listing them.

5. Cells (cells.py): every column of the table has an entry in Row.cells. SourceLocation: kind = RawSheet.source_kind, sheet_name, sheet_index, row_number, column_number, line_number (delimited text) from the raw cell; empty grid positions get their coordinates. extraction_confidence = RawCell.confidence (engine score from future PDF/OCR adapters; copy page_number and bbox into SourceLocation too); it stays None for spreadsheet and delimited-text sources: verbatim digital extraction has no confidence score and must never show an invented 1.0. raw_value = RawCell.text exactly as extracted (never stripped); None for missing cells.
   - Missing cell, EMPTY, or STRING that is empty/whitespace only: data_type EMPTY, parse_status NOT_APPLICABLE (raw_value keeps any whitespace text).
   - NUMBER (typed source value): NumberValue(value = RawCell.number exactly), currency = CurrencyResolver.find_in_number_format(number_format), is_percent when the number format has a % outside quotes and brackets (the value stays as stored: Excel stores 5 % as 0.05); EXACT.
   - BOOLEAN -> BooleanValue; DATETIME -> DateValue for date objects or midnight datetimes with a date-only number format, else DateTimeValue; ERROR -> ErrorValue(code), data_type ERROR; all EXACT. UNAVAILABLE -> data_type UNKNOWN, parse_status UNAVAILABLE, parse_note "Formula has no cached result; formulas are never evaluated." and one warning formula_without_cached_result per table with a count.
   - Formula cells: FormulaInfo(expression = RawCell.formula, cached_result_available = value_type is not UNAVAILABLE); the value is the cached result as above.
   - Numbers that do not fit app.models.values.is_bounded_decimal (more than 28 significant digits or outside 1E-20..1E+25) are FAILED with parse_note "Number outside the supported range" (never construct an invalid NumberValue).
   - STRING in a column mapped to a TEXT field: TextValue(raw text) as parsed_value, normalized_value = whitespace-collapsed NFC text, data_type TEXT, NOT_APPLICABLE; for the field with role unit, normalized_value is the canonical unit_id when UnitCatalog resolves the text ("m²" -> "m2"), else the collapsed text.
   - STRING in a column mapped to a NUMBER field or in an unmapped column: number parsing (6), then dates (ISO 8601 date/datetime always; D.M.Y or M/D/Y per profile.locale.date_order; with date_order auto only when unambiguous: 4-digit year and a day > 12), else text (unmapped: TEXT / NOT_APPLICABLE; NUMBER field: data_type TEXT, parse_status FAILED, parse_note "Not a number: '…'"). In a DATE field, an ambiguous day/month order gives AMBIGUOUS.

6. Number parsing (numbers.py, pure functions, never float):
   - Strip surrounding whitespace incl. NBSP U+00A0, U+202F, U+2009. Accept an optional sign (+, -, U+2212), accounting parentheses "(1.234,50)" or a trailing minus "1234,50-" (negative); an optional currency marker before or after the number resolved by CurrencyResolver.resolve_token ("€ 1.234,50", "1,234.50 EUR"; an unknown word makes the text non-numeric); an optional trailing % (value / 100, is_percent True).
   - Grouping characters: "." "," space (incl. NBSP variants) and apostrophe (' and U+2019). The first group has 1-3 digits, later groups exactly 3.
   - Only digits ("1234", "0012"): EXACT integer. Both "." and "," present: the right-most is the decimal separator, the other grouping (must be valid) -> EXACT. One kind of separator occurring more than once ("1.234.567"): grouping -> EXACT integer (invalid grouping -> not a number). Space/apostrophe grouping with an optional "." or "," decimal ("1 234,56", "1'234.50") -> EXACT. Exactly one "." or "," followed by a number of digits other than 3 ("12,5", "1234.5"), or with a leading group of "0" ("0,125") -> EXACT decimal.
   - Exactly one "." or "," with exactly 3 following digits and a 1-3 digit leading group that is not "0" ("1,234", "1.234", "12.500"): ambiguous on its own. Resolve with evidence, in order: (a) the column's separator from its other cells that unambiguously used "." or "," as decimal separator or as grouping, when that evidence agrees -> INFERRED with parse_note citing it ("Decimal separator ',' inferred from 14 other values in this column"); (b) the document-level separator when every column with evidence agrees; (c) profile.locale.decimal_separator when not "auto" (note cites the profile); (d) otherwise AMBIGUOUS: no value, data_type UNKNOWN, parse_note giving both readings ("Could be 1.234 or 1234"). Conflicting evidence within a column leaves its ambiguous cells AMBIGUOUS and adds warning decimal_separator_conflict. Typed XLSX numbers are never separator evidence. Unresolved ambiguous cells produce one warning number_format_ambiguous per table with a count. Column.decimal_separator records the column's resolved separator (None without evidence).
   - Not numbers: "approx. 5", "5-10", "1.2.3", "n/a", "incl.", "-", digit strings longer than 30 digits, anything non-finite.

7. Column type profiles (types.py): counts by data_type over the non-empty cells of data rows; dominant_type = most frequent type when its share >= options.type_dominance_threshold, else MIXED; empty column: counts {}, non_empty 0, dominant_type EMPTY, share 0.0; Column.inferred_type = dominant_type.

8. Row classification (rows.py) per profile.row_classification (read the RowClassificationRules docstring):
   - Label text: the first non-empty mapped cell among label_fields, else (fallback_to_first_text_cell) the first text cell of the row; NFKC, whitespace collapsed, truncated to options.max_label_chars before matching; patterns use re.search with IGNORECASE, compiled once per profile (cache), never per row.
   - Order: BLANK (no non-empty cells) -> GROSS_TOTAL -> TAX -> GRAND_TOTAL -> SUBTOTAL -> carry-forward (kind TEXT, rule "pattern.carry_forward") -> ITEM when any item_value_fields cell is non-empty in a numeric sense (NUMBER, or AMBIGUOUS/FAILED in a numeric field) or the field with role unit is non-empty -> SECTION when a section pattern matches the label, or section_code_pattern matches the identifier-role field's text -> TEXT.
   - Generic profiles without item_value_fields: every non-blank row that no pattern claims is an ITEM.
   - RowClassification.rule is one of: blank, pattern.gross_total, pattern.tax, pattern.grand_total, pattern.subtotal, pattern.carry_forward, values.item, unit.item, pattern.section, code.section, text, default.item; matched_text holds the matched substring (<= 120 chars).
   - Section hierarchy (read the Row and RowClassification docstrings): RowClassification.section_level for SECTION rows = number of code segments of the section's identifier code when it has one ("01" -> 1, "01.02" -> 2), else 1 + the level of the innermost open section whose label was matched by a different section pattern... keep it simple and deterministic: code depth when a code exists, otherwise 1. A SECTION row at level L closes every open section with level >= L and becomes the innermost open section. Row.section_path = ids of the open sections, outermost first (a SECTION row's path lists only its ancestors). A SUBTOTAL row: if its identifier cell or its label contains a code (a dotted numeric code such as 01 or 01.02) that equals (by segments with leading zeros removed) the code of an open section, Row.closes_section_id = that section and it and every deeper open section are closed after the row; otherwise, if there is an open section, it closes the innermost one; otherwise closes_section_id stays None. GRAND_TOTAL / GROSS_TOTAL rows close all open sections (their scope is the whole table; closes_section_id stays None). Never guess beyond these rules; detectors abstain when scope is None.
   - Totals treatment: for ITEM rows, when any profile not_in_totals_patterns pattern matches the label text or the text of a mapped field with role category, set RowClassification.counts_toward_totals=False and totals_rule="pattern.not_in_totals"; everything else counts.

9. Quality (warnings.py: one documented table of codes): every ExtractionWarning carries needs_review. Needs review: header_uncertain, header_ambiguous, number_format_ambiguous, decimal_separator_conflict, formula_without_cached_result, mapping_rejected_type_mismatch, required_field_unmapped, and adapter codes encoding_guessed, ragged_rows, single_column_file. Informational (needs_review=False): hidden_sheet_skipped, empty_sheet_skipped, chartsheet_skipped, mapping_conflict, macro_project_ignored, external_links_ignored, decisions_applied. Re-flag adapter warnings from this table (evolve them); unknown codes keep needs_review=True (fail safe). ExtractionReport.quality = NEEDS_REVIEW when any warning needs review, else OK.

9b. Reviewer decisions (app/models/schema.py SchemaDecisions, optional build_dataset argument): for a sheet with TableDecisions, header_row_numbers replaces detection (method USER; [] means no header), column_fields replaces automatic mapping for the listed source columns (method USER, similarity None; None unmaps), row_kinds overrides classification for listed source rows (rule "user"), not_data_rows become Row.excluded=True. Decisions referring to rows/columns that do not exist are ignored with an informational warning decisions_applied that reports what was applied and what was ignored. Apply decisions before row classification and section building so everything downstream reflects them.

10. Ids from the injected IdFactory with IdPrefix (DATASET, TABLE, COLUMN, ROW), created_at from the injected Clock, revision 1, profile id/version from the profile. Never mutate the RawWorkbook. Linear time in the number of cells: a 20,000-row x 8-column sheet must normalize in a few seconds.

Model invariants enforced on construction (read backend/app/models/dataset.py): one column per semantic field per table, exactly one cell per column in every row, unique ids, ascending header_row_numbers.

TESTS (backend/tests/unit/normalization/): table-driven number parsing (every rule above, German and English formats, negatives, currency, percent, rejects); separator evidence (column-level, document-level, profile locale, conflict, unresolved -> AMBIGUOUS with no value); header detection (English BoQ "Item | Description | Qty | Unit | Rate | Amount", German LV "OZ | Kurztext | Menge | ME | EP | GP" with title rows above, two-row header with a merged "Preis" cell over "EP" and "GP", structural, first-row and none fallbacks, ambiguity); mapping (exact, contains, fuzzy threshold, short keys, one-to-one, conflict, type-mismatch rejection); cells (typed values, formulas with and without cached results, percent, currency from number formats, delimited-text line numbers, whitespace-only cells); row classification for German and English BoQs ("Titel 01 Erdarbeiten", items, "Summe Titel 01", "Übertrag", "Gesamtsumme netto", "zzgl. 19 % MwSt." / "MwSt. 19 %", "Gesamtsumme brutto"; "Subtotal", "Total carried to collection", "Grand total", "VAT 20%", "Total incl. VAT"; an item whose description starts with "Section" but has values stays an item; long-text continuation lines become text) and the generic profile; section hierarchy (nested "Titel 01" > "01.01" / "01.02" with "Summe 01.01", "Summe 01.02", "Summe Titel 01" getting the right section_path and closes_section_id; flat English sections; subtotals without any section); not-in-totals items ("Bedarfsposition", "nur EP", "Alternative item", an item-type column "Wahl"); reviewer decisions; extraction_confidence None for XLSX/CSV and copied for raw cells that carry a confidence; warnings and quality; ExtractionError for an all-empty workbook; determinism (same input + SequentialIdFactory + FixedClock -> equal datasets); the RawWorkbook is not mutated; a 20,000-row performance test marked with the existing pytest marker "evaluation" if it takes longer than 2 s (check backend/pyproject.toml markers).`

const ARITHMETIC = `${SHARED}

${DETECTOR_CONVENTIONS}

YOUR OWNERSHIP: backend/app/validation/deterministic/{line_total,subtotal,grand_total,currency_consistency}.py, optional private helpers in backend/app/validation/deterministic/_arithmetic_support.py, and tests backend/tests/unit/validation/deterministic/test_{line_total,subtotal,grand_total,currency_consistency}.py (plus a shared test helper module if needed, e.g. tests/unit/validation/deterministic/arithmetic_fixtures.py). Nothing else. All arithmetic goes through decimal_math (multiply, add_all, subtract, RoundingSpec, TolerancePolicy, power_of_ten_factor); never float.

DETECTORS:
1. deterministic.line_total ("Line total = quantity × unit price", category arithmetic). Config: quantity_field="quantity", price_field="unit_price", total_field="line_total", unit_field="unit", rounding: RoundingSpec (default 2 dp, half_up), tolerance: TolerancePolicy (default absolute 0.01), infer_precision_from_column = True, skip_unit_dimensions: list[str] = [], severity = high, detect_scaling_errors = True. For each item row with usable quantity, price and total: factor = the resolved unit's quantity_factor (the BoQ profile sets 0.01 for %, so "5 % of 100,000.00" gives 5,000.00) unless the quantity cell itself is a percent value (NumberValue.is_percent: already divided by 100, factor 1); exact = multiply(q, factor, p); use decimal_math.reconcile(total, exact, rounding=..., tolerance=...), which accepts the exact or the rounded recomputation and reports which convention matched. infer_precision_from_column: when every usable reported line total of a table has at most k decimal places with k below rounding.decimal_places (e.g. whole-euro estimates), round to k places for that table and say so in the evidence. A mismatch -> confirmed_rule_violation with observed = stated total, expected = recomputed, a CALCULATION evidence item with a typed Calculation (operation "product", operands quantity/unit factor/unit price with their cells, exact_result, rounding, rounded_result, reported, difference, allowed_difference) and a readable detail ("12.5 × 40.00 = 500.000 → 500.00 (2 decimal places, half up); stated 5,000.00; difference 4,500.00; allowed ±0.01"), RULE evidence, inputs = the quantity and unit price cells, highlighted cell = the line total, financial impact kind "discrepancy" (amount = stated - recomputed, reported, recomputed). When detect_scaling_errors and power_of_ten_factor(total, exact) returns k, add a NOTE ("The stated total is about 10^k times the recomputed value, which suggests a decimal-point or scaling error.") as context, never as proof. Skip with coverage counters: rows missing an input (rows_skipped_missing_inputs; an item with only a unit price, i.e. "nur EP", is normal), rows with unusable inputs, rows whose unit resolves to a dimension in skip_unit_dimensions, rows whose explicit currency markers on price and total disagree (the currency detector reports those), ArithmeticError (rows_skipped_out_of_range). Coverage also: rows_checked, rows_consistent, rows_inconsistent.
2. deterministic.subtotal ("Subtotal reconciliation", category subtotal). Config: total_field="line_total", identifier_field="item_code", tolerance (default absolute 0.01, per_term_absolute 0.005; the BoQ profile sets these), use_code_prefix_scopes=True, label_code_pattern (default matches a dotted numeric code such as 01, 01.02 or 3.1.20 in the subtotal label; exactly one capture group), severity = high, max_reference_cells = 50. For each SUBTOTAL row (not excluded) with a usable stated amount, build candidate scopes:
   (0) section scope: when normalization set Row.closes_section_id, the scope is the item rows before the subtotal whose section_path contains that section. This is the primary scope; when it exists, (a) and (b) are not used.
   (a) code scope (use_code_prefix_scopes and identifier mapped): the subtotal's code is its own identifier cell, else the first label_code_pattern match in its label (description or first text cell). Scope = item rows before the subtotal row whose identifier code_segments (_support.code_segments) start with the subtotal code's segments. Only a candidate when it contains at least one item.
   (b) block scope: item rows after the nearest preceding row of kind SUBTOTAL, GRAND_TOTAL, TAX or GROSS_TOTAL (or the table start) and before the subtotal. Only a candidate when it contains at least one item.
   Summands are the REPORTED line totals (never recomputed values, so one wrong line is not reported twice) of items with counts_toward_totals=True; items with counts_toward_totals=False are left out and listed in a NOTE; rows marked not data are never summands. A scope is complete when every counted item in it has a usable line total and the summands' explicit currencies do not conflict; incomplete scopes cannot be verified (abstain, never "sum the rest"). If any complete scope reconciles within tolerance.allowed_difference(expected, terms=len(scope)) -> consistent. Else if the complete candidates are exactly one, or all have the same membership -> confirmed_rule_violation (CALCULATION: sum of N line totals = X; stated Y; difference; allowed ±Z with the per-term part explained; REFERENCE evidence with the summed cells, capped, full count in data; NOTE naming the scope rule, e.g. "items whose code starts with 01.02" or "items between rows 12 and 40"); financial impact stated - expected. Else (several complete scopes with different members and none reconciles) -> extraction_uncertainty, severity medium, explaining that the subtotal matches neither reading of the section structure. No candidate scope (e.g. nested subtotals without codes) or only incomplete scopes -> no issue; coverage subtotals_skipped_no_scope / subtotals_skipped_incomplete_scope and a note. Coverage also: subtotals_checked, subtotals_consistent, subtotals_inconsistent, subtotals_skipped_missing_amount. Nested BoQs must work through code scopes: "Summe 01.01", "Summe 01.02" then "Summe Titel 01" (sums all items 01.x). Display rounding: config display_rounding_drift_per_term (default 0.005) applies only to tables whose source kind is delimited_text (printed exports where each line was rounded for display while the printed total was computed from unrounded values): a difference beyond the tolerance but within tolerance + drift × terms produces a severity-info extraction_uncertainty finding ("difference explainable by rounding of N printed values") instead of a violation. Applicability: skip with a reason when no table has SUBTOTAL rows or the total field is unmapped.
3. deterministic.grand_total ("Grand total and tax reconciliation", category grand_total). Config: total_field="line_total", tolerance (default absolute 0.01, per_term_absolute 0.005), check_tax_lines=True, tax_rate_pattern (default captures a rate such as 19 or 19,0 or 7.5 before a % sign; one capture group; decimal comma or point), tax_rounding: RoundingSpec (2 dp half_up), check_gross_totals=True, severity = high. For each GRAND_TOTAL row with a usable amount: scope = counted item rows after the previous GRAND_TOTAL or GROSS_TOTAL row (or the table start), independent of subtotals; summands, completeness, currency and display-rounding rules exactly as for subtotals (share the code in _arithmetic_support.py); complete scopes are compared with terms=len(scope) (confirmed_rule_violation with CALCULATION/REFERENCE/RULE evidence and financial impact), incomplete scopes are skipped with coverage. TAX rows (check_tax_lines) following a grand total: when the label contains a rate, expected tax = tax_rounding.apply(net × rate / 100) using the nearest preceding grand total as net; mismatch -> confirmed_rule_violation; no rate -> coverage tax_rows_without_rate. GROSS_TOTAL rows (check_gross_totals): expected = nearest preceding grand total + the tax rows between them (all usable) -> mismatch is a violation. Coverage counters for every skip reason. Applicability: needs at least one GRAND_TOTAL, TAX or GROSS_TOTAL row and the mapped total field.
4. deterministic.currency_consistency ("Currency consistency", category currency). Config: expected_currency: str | None = None (ISO 4217, validated), fields: list[str] | None = None (None = every profile field with role monetary that is mapped), currency_field: str | None = None (None = the mapped field with role currency, if any), severity = medium, max_cells_per_issue = 50. Explicit markers only: NumberValue.currency, Column.header_currency, and a per-row currency column value resolved with context.currencies.resolve_token. Unmarked amounts are never flagged and never assumed to be in a default currency. Rules: (a) row conflict: two monetary cells of one row (or a cell and its row's currency column) carry different explicit currencies -> confirmed_rule_violation for that row; (b) expected_currency set: explicit markers that differ -> confirmed_rule_violation, grouped per (column, currency); (c) without expected_currency and without a currency column: more than one explicit currency among a table's monetary cells -> one issue per minority currency (fewest cells; ties broken by first appearance) not already reported in (a), grouped with capped cells. With a currency column, mixed currencies across rows are legitimate (only (a) and (b) apply). Coverage: cells_with_explicit_currency, cells_without_currency.

TESTS also: German formats (values already parsed as Decimal in the builders), lump-sum rows (1 psch, EP = GP), percent items (qty 5, unit %, rate 100,000.00, amount 5,000.00 passes; no scaling note), whole-euro tables with column precision inference, exact-vs-rounded conventions, not-counted items left out of sums, section scopes from closes_section_id (use the builder's level= / closes_section= / in_totals= options), CSV display-rounding drift producing an info finding, zero quantity, tolerance boundaries (difference exactly at the allowed amount passes, one cent beyond fails), half-up vs half-even rounding differences, a 10x scaling error note, nested sections with code scopes, a block-scope BoQ without codes, an ambiguous-scope case producing extraction_uncertainty, incomplete scopes abstaining, VAT 19 % and 7 % lines, gross totals, multiple grand totals in one table, currency conflicts in a row, minority currency, expected_currency, unmarked amounts producing nothing, reviewer-excluded rows ignored as terms and as totals, and that low-confidence cells (number_cell(..., confidence=0.5)) downgrade confirmed findings to extraction_uncertainty through the factory.`

const QUALITY = `${SHARED}

${DETECTOR_CONVENTIONS}

YOUR OWNERSHIP: backend/app/validation/deterministic/{required_values,data_type_conformance,numeric_validity,field_constraints,exact_duplicates,extraction_quality}.py, optional private helpers in backend/app/validation/deterministic/_quality_support.py, and tests backend/tests/unit/validation/deterministic/test_{required_values,data_type_conformance,numeric_validity,field_constraints,exact_duplicates,extraction_quality}.py. Nothing else. Responsibilities must not overlap: each kind of problem is reported by exactly one detector (missing -> required_values; text in a numeric field -> data_type_conformance; numeric bounds/integers/decimals/zero -> numeric_validity; pattern/allowed values/uniqueness -> field_constraints; identical rows -> exact_duplicates; ambiguous/unavailable/low-confidence cells and header/mapping uncertainty -> extraction_quality; spreadsheet error literals belong to another engineer's formula_errors detector).

DETECTORS:
1. deterministic.required_values ("Missing required values", category missing_value). Config: fields: list[str] | None = None (None = profile fields with required=True), report_unmapped_fields = True, severity_by_field: dict[str, Severity] = {} (default severity: high for fields with role quantity or monetary, medium otherwise), lump_sum_quantity_optional = True (a row whose unit resolves to dimension lump_sum may omit the quantity), max_row_issues_per_field = 50, unit_field = "unit". For each table and each row whose kind is in the field's required_row_kinds (default item rows) where the mapped cell is EMPTY -> confirmed_rule_violation ("Missing unit price"; explanation names row and field; the affected cell is the empty cell; fingerprint_key = field id; suggested action asks to fill it in or confirm it is intentionally blank). Cells that are UNAVAILABLE, AMBIGUOUS or FAILED are not missing (other detectors report them). A missing line total (or other monetary field) on an item with counts_toward_totals=False (e.g. "nur EP") is intentional and not reported. When more than max_row_issues_per_field rows of one table miss the same field, report one grouped issue for that field instead (count, first source rows listed, capped cells), e.g. an unpriced tender where every unit price is blank. Unmapped required fields (report_unmapped_fields): one table-level issue per field, evidence extraction_uncertainty, severity medium, "Required column 'Unit price' not found", explaining that checks depending on it could not run and the column mapping should be reviewed. Applicability: always applicable when the profile has required fields.
2. deterministic.data_type_conformance ("Invalid data types", category data_type). Config: fields: list[str] | None = None (None = every mapped field with data_type number, date or boolean), check_unmapped_columns = True, dominance_threshold = 0.95, min_non_empty = 10, max_cells_per_issue = 50. (a) Mapped fields: cells of item rows and total rows whose parse_status is FAILED or whose data_type differs from the field's data type (ignoring EMPTY, ERROR, AMBIGUOUS and UNAVAILABLE, which other detectors report) -> confirmed_rule_violation medium, per cell ("Quantity is not a number: 'approx. 5'"), parse_note as evidence. More than max_cells_per_issue in one column -> one grouped issue for that column. (b) Unmapped columns (check_unmapped_columns): when the column's type_profile has dominant_share >= dominance_threshold, dominant_type not TEXT/MIXED/EMPTY and non_empty >= min_non_empty, the minority-typed cells form one grouped issue per column: evidence suspected_anomaly, severity low ("3 of 120 values in column 'Menge 2' are not numbers"), clearly worded as an unusual observation, not an error, with no score.
3. deterministic.numeric_validity ("Invalid numerical values", category numeric_value). Config: constraints: dict[str, FieldConstraints] = {} (replaces the profile's constraints for that field id), fields: list[str] | None = None (None = number fields that have constraints). For usable numbers in item rows check minimum/maximum (with exclusive flags), integer_only, max_decimal_places (places of the normalized Decimal, so 12.50 has 1 significant decimal place and 12.505 has 3; document this), allow_zero. Each failure -> confirmed_rule_violation (minimum/maximum: medium; integer_only, max_decimal_places, zero: low) with RULE evidence stating the constraint and its source (profile or plan), fingerprint_key "<field>:<constraint>". The BoQ profile flags negative quantities and unit prices. Pattern, allowed_values and unique are NOT checked here.
4. deterministic.field_constraints ("Domain constraints", category constraint). Config: constraints: dict[str, FieldConstraints] = {} (replaces profile constraints per field), max_cells_per_issue = 50, case_sensitive_unique = False. Text constraints on item rows: pattern (re.fullmatch, IGNORECASE, on the normalized text; text truncated to 1,000 characters before matching) -> confirmed_rule_violation low; allowed_values (case-insensitive after whitespace collapse) -> medium; unique (among item rows of one table; comparison key _support.normalize_code for fields with role identifier, else case-folded collapsed text, or exact text when case_sensitive_unique) -> one issue per duplicate group, confirmed_rule_violation medium ("Item code 01.02.0030 appears 3 times"), row_id = the second occurrence, affected cells = the code cells of every row in the group (capped), REFERENCE evidence listing all source rows, fingerprint_key = the comparison key. Numeric bounds are NOT checked here.
5. deterministic.exact_duplicates ("Exact duplicate rows", category duplicate). Config: ignore_fields: list[str] = [], ignore_columns: list[str] = [] (normalized column names), case_sensitive = False, min_non_empty_cells = 2, max_cells_per_issue = 50, total_field = "line_total". Key per item row over every column of the table except ignored ones: numbers by Decimal value (normalize(), so 12.50 equals 12.5), text case-folded and whitespace-collapsed unless case_sensitive, unit cells by their normalized value, empty as None; rows with fewer than min_non_empty_cells non-empty compared cells are skipped. Groups of >= 2 rows -> one issue per group: confirmed_rule_violation medium ("Rows 14 and 27 are identical"), row_id = the second occurrence, affected cells from all rows (capped), REFERENCE evidence with every source row, fingerprint_key = a hash of the key. When the total field is mapped and usable on counted rows, add financial impact kind "potential_double_count" = the sum of the line totals of the extra counted rows, basis "potential double counting of N duplicated row(s)" (explicit currency only). Rows that are identical except for the item code are NOT exact duplicates.
6. deterministic.extraction_quality ("Extraction uncertainty", category extraction). Config: confidence_threshold = 0.9, report_header_uncertainty = True, report_fuzzy_mappings = True, fields: list[str] | None = None (None = all mapped fields), max_issues_per_column = 200. Every non-blank row: mapped cells with AMBIGUOUS parse status -> extraction_uncertainty medium ("Ambiguous number '1.234' in Quantity", parse_note as evidence, suggested action to confirm the decimal separator); UNAVAILABLE -> extraction_uncertainty medium ("Formula result not stored in the file"; evidence includes the formula expression; formulas are never evaluated); extraction_confidence (present only for OCR/PDF sources; None for XLSX/CSV, which must not produce any confidence finding) below confidence_threshold -> extraction_uncertainty (low below the threshold, medium below half of it) with the confidence value and threshold in evidence data. More than max_issues_per_column in one column -> one grouped issue per column and problem type. report_header_uncertainty: table.header.method STRUCTURAL / FIRST_ROW / NONE -> one table-level issue, extraction_uncertainty medium, explaining the header was not recognized from the profile and the column mapping should be reviewed. report_fuzzy_mappings: each column mapped with method header_fuzzy -> one low-severity table-level issue naming header, field and the similarity (a string-similarity ratio, explicitly not a probability). FAILED cells are not reported here.

TESTS also: German BoQ examples, lump-sum rows without quantity, unpriced-tender grouping, items vs total rows, excluded rows ignored, generic profile behaviour, grouping thresholds, and no overlap between detectors on the same defective cell (build one dataset with each defect and assert exactly one detector reports each).`

const REFERENCES_UNITS = `${SHARED}

${DETECTOR_CONVENTIONS}

YOUR OWNERSHIP: backend/app/validation/deterministic/{formula_errors,reference_integrity,unit_vocabulary,unit_compatibility}.py, optional private helpers in backend/app/validation/deterministic/_reference_support.py, and tests backend/tests/unit/validation/deterministic/test_{formula_errors,reference_integrity,unit_vocabulary,unit_compatibility}.py. Nothing else. Regex parameters come from profiles or plans and are applied to untrusted cell text: compile once per run, bound the text length before matching (config max_text_chars, default 2,000), and validate capture-group counts in the config models.

DETECTORS:
1. deterministic.formula_errors ("Spreadsheet formula errors", category formula_error). Config: flag_external_references = True, severity = high, max_cells_per_issue = 50. Every cell of every non-blank row (all columns, mapped or not) whose value is an ErrorValue (#REF!, #DIV/0!, #VALUE!, #NAME?, #N/A, #NUM!, #NULL!, …) -> confirmed_rule_violation ("Formula error #REF! in Line total"; VALUE evidence with the error code and the formula expression when present; the spreadsheet itself reports the error), one issue per cell, grouped per (column, error code) beyond max_cells_per_issue. External references (flag_external_references): formulas that reference another workbook ("=[1]Sheet1!A1", "='C:\\path\\[Book.xlsx]Sheet'!A1", "=[Book.xlsx]Sheet!A1") -> needs_external_context, severity low, grouped per column ("12 values in Line total are computed from another workbook"), explaining that the value cannot be verified from this document. Formulas without cached results are NOT reported here (extraction_quality owns them). Applicable to every dataset.
2. deterministic.reference_integrity ("Reference integrity", category reference). Config: rules: list[FieldReferenceRule] = [] (rule_id, source_field, target_field, separator: str | None = None to split several references in one cell), text_references: list[TextReferenceRule] = [] (rule_id, text_field, pattern with exactly one capture group, target_field) - the BoQ profile already defines two text_references rules in detector_defaults; your config must accept them unchanged; severity_field_rule = medium, severity_text_rule = low, max_text_chars = 2000. Known identifiers = target_field texts of item rows across ALL tables of the dataset, compared by _support.code_segments ("01.02.0030" matches "1.2.30"). Field rules: each referenced code that matches no known identifier -> confirmed_rule_violation (the document declares a reference that does not resolve). Text references: each captured code that matches no known identifier -> needs_external_context ("The description refers to position 01.02.0030, which is not in this document"; it may be in another document or lot); a row referring to its own code is ignored; resolved references produce nothing. Applicability: skipped with a reason when no rules are configured or none of their fields are mapped.
3. deterministic.unit_vocabulary ("Unit vocabulary", category unit). Config: unit_field = "unit", severity = low, max_cells_per_issue = 200. For item rows with non-empty unit text that context.units.resolve() cannot resolve: one issue per distinct unknown unit (grouped by profiles.units.unit_key) -> needs_external_context ("Unit 'VE' is not in the profile's unit vocabulary (12 rows)"), capped cells, count in evidence, suggested action to correct the unit or add an alias to the profile. Applicability: skipped when the unit field is unmapped or the profile defines no units.
4. deterministic.unit_compatibility ("Unit compatibility", category unit). Config: description_rules: list[UnitDescriptionRule] = [] (rule_id, description_pattern, allowed_dimensions: non-empty list of dimension names, rationale) - the BoQ profile already defines two in detector_defaults; check_integer_quantities = True, check_lump_sum_quantity = True, check_same_description_dimension = True, min_description_words = 3, description_field = "description", quantity_field = "quantity", unit_field = "unit", max_cells_per_issue = 50, max_text_chars = 2000. All findings are semantic_warning (advisory; the brief says unit rules apply only when the category is sufficiently established): (a) a description matching a rule while the row's unit resolves to a dimension outside allowed_dimensions -> medium, with the rule's rationale and the matched words as evidence; (b) check_integer_quantities: the unit has integer_quantities and the usable quantity has a fractional part -> low; (c) check_lump_sum_quantity: unit dimension lump_sum with a usable quantity other than 1 -> low; (d) check_same_description_dimension: item rows (all tables) with the same normalized description (NFKC, case-folded, whitespace-collapsed, at least min_description_words words) whose resolved units span more than one dimension -> one issue per description group, medium, affected unit cells from all rows (capped). Rows with unknown or empty units are ignored by every rule (unit_vocabulary and required_values report those). Applicability: unit field mapped and a unit vocabulary present.

TESTS also: German and English descriptions ("Stundenlohnarbeiten Facharbeiter" in m³, "Baustelleneinrichtung" in m², "Daywork labour" in h passes), unit aliases (m², qm, Stk., psch), 2.5 pcs, 3 psch, identical descriptions in m² and pcs, unknown unit grouping, external workbook formulas, every error literal, references in German ("wie Pos. 01.02.0030", "siehe OZ 3.1.20") and English ("as item 2.1.4"), references with leading zeros resolving, references spanning tables, self references, pattern length bounding (a 100,000-character description must not be slow).`

const SCHEMA = {
  type: 'object',
  properties: {
    report: { type: 'string', description: 'the concise final report described in the instructions' },
    tests_passed: { type: 'boolean', description: 'true only if every test you ran passed in the final run' },
    contract_change_requests: { type: 'array', items: { type: 'string' } },
  },
  required: ['report', 'tests_passed', 'contract_change_requests'],
}

const PACKAGES = {
  normalization: NORMALIZATION,
  arithmetic: ARITHMETIC,
  quality: QUALITY,
  references_units: REFERENCES_UNITS,
}

const selected = (args && args.packages) || Object.keys(PACKAGES)
phase('Build')
const results = await parallel(selected.map(key => () =>
  agent(PACKAGES[key], { label: `build:${key}`, phase: 'Build', schema: SCHEMA }).then(r => ({ pkg: key, ...r }))))
return results.filter(Boolean)
